You are given an integer array nums and an integer target.
You want to build an expression out of nums by adding one of the symbols '+' and '-' before each integer in nums and
then concatenate all the integers.
For example, if nums = [2, 1], you can add a '+' before 2 and a '-' before 1 and concatenate them to build the
expression "+2-1". Return the number of different expressions that you can build, which evaluates to target.

Example 1:
Input: nums = [1,1,1,1,1], target = 3
Output: 5
Explanation: There are 5 ways to assign symbols to make the sum of nums be target 3.
-1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 3
+1 - 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 3
+1 + 1 - 1 + 1 + 1 = 3
+1 + 1 + 1 - 1 + 1 = 3
+1 + 1 + 1 + 1 - 1 = 3

Example 2:
Input: nums = [1], target = 1
Output: 1

Constraints:

1 <= nums.length <= 20
0 <= nums[i] <= 1000
0 <= sum(nums[i]) <= 1000
-1000 <= target <= 1000

Approach 1:
Is the backward approach where we start with the problem having n numbers and curr_sum = 0 to reach the target. Here
pick or not pick style won't work as there is a requirement to include all the numbers of the array and assign them
each with + or - sign. The base case to find the target sum depends on both the n going to 0 and the curr_sum reaching
the target. If the elements are covered before reaching the required target then there is no way from this branch. When
building the dp we need to keep in mind that the index might go -ve and hence we need to consider the size of the dp in
the number of columns to be 2 * total_sum_of_array such that we can shift the -ve indexes by just adding the
total_sum_of_array to each index. In tabulation a special carefulness needs to be shown in handling indexes that might
go out of bound. Finally, the answer lies at dp[n][totalSum] which is according to the definition of the state of each
cell of our dp and to the problem statement that we start to solve.

Naive approach -> TC = O(2^n), SC = O(n) (recursion stack space)
Each call makes up to 2 recursive calls, and every recursive call can goes n levels deep.
Memoization -> TC = O(n * totalSum), SC = O(n * totalSum) + O(n) (recursion stack space) = O(n * totalSum)
Tabulation -> TC = O(n * totalSum), SC = O(n * totalSum)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation -> TC = O(n * totalSum), SC = O(2 * totalSum) = O(totalSum)

Approach 2:
Is the forward approach where we start with the problem having n numbers and p = the value deduced from the reduced eqn.
There are two mathematical equations that can be derived from this problem statement,
    1. P + N = total
    2. P - N = target
Here,
    P -> subset corresponding to the sum of positive numbers.
    N -> subset corresponding to the sum of negative numbers.
The idea is in order to have a way to assign all the elements of the given array with a sign and then have their total
equalise to the target both these equations have to be satisfied. So when these equations are reduced meaning when added
a new eqn is formed,
    2P = total + target -> P = (total + target)/2
So we simply need to find this P first and then find how many ways it can be reached or we can say the problem boils
down to how many subsets are there which can form P. This in turn satisfies our two initial equations as P is derived
from them and hence our given problem is solved.
We also need to be careful with the value of P as it can be -ve (then the P according to the eqns will not stand correct)
or not be a fractional number (this way it will be never be able to reach) and has to be a whole number. To check whether
it is whole or not we simply can check if the sum of (total + target) is even or odd. If odd it is not a whole number.
Here we use the pick and not pick recursive calls with proper checks and the required base cases.

Naive approach -> TC = O(2^n), SC = O(n) (recursion stack space)
Each call makes up to 2 recursive calls, and every recursive call can goes n levels deep.
Memoization -> TC = O(n * P), SC = O(n * P) + O(n) (recursion stack space) = O(n * P)
Tabulation -> TC = O(n * P), SC = O(n * P)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation -> TC = O(n * P), SC = O(2 * P) = O(P)